  Behavior is preserved: empty username or password still blocks the [38;5;153mlogin()[39m call and reports to the console. The only
  user-visible difference is that the console now names which fields failed instead of printing one "Missing required
  fields". Error [3mdisplay[0m stays out of the module, per your scope choice — this form keeps logging, exactly as it does
  now.

  [1mTesting.[0m This is the tooling question, and it's the last open decision. The repo has no test runner, no linter, and no
  dependencies. Since we chose the Node-importable module style specifically so validation could be tested, I'd use
  [1m[38;5;153mnode:test[0m — built into Node, so [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gains a [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m script and still zero dependencies. Tests
  would cover: [38;5;153mrequired[39m on empty/whitespace/present values, [38;5;153mvalidate[39m returning all failing fields at once,
  first-failure-per-field, a custom function rule, and [38;5;153mreadFormValues[39m preferring [38;5;153mname[39m over [38;5;153mid[39m. [38;5;153mreadFormValues[39m gets a
  tiny hand-rolled fake form object rather than a DOM library — it only calls [38;5;153mquerySelectorAll[39m, so the fake is a few
  lines and keeps the dependency count at zero.

  I'm deliberately not proposing a linter or formatter here; a four-file repo with no config doesn't benefit enough to
  justify the churn, and adding one would reformat files unrelated to this task. Say the word if you want one anyway.

  Two things to confirm before I write the spec:
